ive exponent to a positive exponent by taking the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{-1} &= \\frac{1}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{x^3} = \\dfrac 1 x\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186063919 1074 "We subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}} & = \\\\ {x}^{(2-3)} & = \\\\ {x}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we convert the negative exponent to a positive exponent by taking the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{x^3} = \\dfrac 1 x.\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186063920 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{z^5}{z^{-3} }.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186063920 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{z^5}{z^{-3}}$." 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186063920 1074 "We subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{z^{5}}{z^{-3}} &=  \\\\\nz^{5 - (-3)} &=  \\\\\nz^{5 + 3} &=  \\\\\n&= z^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186063920 1074 "We subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{5}}{{z}^{-3}} & = \\\\ {z}^{5-(-3)} & = \\\\ {z}^{5+3} & = \\\\ {z}^{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186063921 1071 "Simplify the  expression $\\dfrac {p^7} {p^2}  \\cdot p^3 .$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186063921 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac {p^7} {p^2} \\cdot p^3$." 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186063921 1074 "First we apply the quotient rule, subtracting exponents in the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{p^{7}}{p^{2}} \\cdot p^{3} &=  \\\\\np^{(7 - 2)} \\cdot p^{3} &=  \\\\\n&= p^{5} \\cdot p^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we apply the product rule, adding exponents in the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{5} \\cdot p^{3} &=  \\\\\np^{(5 + 3)} &=  \\\\\n&= p^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p^{7}}{p^{2}} \\cdot p^{3} = p^{8}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186063921 1074 "First we apply the quotient rule, subtracting exponents in the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{p}^{7}}{{p}^{2}}\\cdot {p}^{3} & = \\\\ {p}^{(7-2)}\\cdot {p}^{3} & = \\\\ {p}^{5}\\cdot {p}^{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we apply the product rule, adding exponents in the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{p}^{5}\\cdot {p}^{3} & = \\\\ {p}^{(5+3)} & = \\\\ {p}^{8}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{p}^{7}}{{p}^{2}}\\cdot {p}^{3}={p}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186063922 1071 "Find the value of $\\left(x^4\\right)^5$." 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186063922 1071 "Find the value of $\\left(x^4\\right)^5.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186063922 1074 "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\left(x^4\\right)^5 \\\\ &= x^{4\\cdot5} \\\\ &= x^{20} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186063922 1074 "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\left(x^4\\right)^5\t&= \\\n\tx^{4\\cdot5} \t&= \\\n\tx^{20}\n}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186063923 1071 "If $\\left(x^{-3}\\right)^{-6} = x^n$, then what is $n$?" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186063923 1071 "If $\\left(x^{-3}\\right)^{-6} = x^n,$ then what is $n?$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186063923 1074 "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\left(x^{-3}\\right)^{-6} \\\\ &= x^{(-3)(-6)} \\\\ &= x^{18} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $n=18$." 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186063923 1074 "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\left(x^{-3}\\right)^{-6}&=\\\n\tx^{(-3)(-6)}&=\\\n\tx^{18}\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore $n=18.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186063924 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt[4]{x^{-8}}$." 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186063924 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt[4]{x^{-8}}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186063924 1074 "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[4]{x^{-8}} &=  \\\\\n(x^{-8})^{1/4} &=  \\\\\nx^{(-8) \\cdot (1/4)} &=  \\\\\nx^{-2} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186063924 1074 "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{-8}} & = \\\\ ({x}^{-8})^{1/4} & = \\\\ {x}^{(-8)\\cdot (1/4)} & = \\\\ {x}^{-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186063925 1071 "If $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\right)^4 \\cdot x = x^k$ for some constant $k$, then what is the value of $k$?" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186063925 1071 "If $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\right)^4 \\cdot x = x^k$ for some constant $k$, then what is the value of $k?$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186063925 1074 "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x}})^{4} \\cdot x &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{1}{x^{1/3}})^{4} \\cdot x &=  \\\\\n(x^{-1/3})^{4} \\cdot x &=  \\\\\nx^{(-1/3) \\cdot (4)} \\cdot x &=  \\\\\n&= x^{-4/3} \\cdot x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule, adding the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{-4/3} \\cdot x &=  \\\\\nx^{-4/3} \\cdot x^{1} &=  \\\\\nx^{-4/3 + 1} &=  \\\\\nx^{-4/3 + 3/3} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{-1/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\nk=-\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186063925 1074 "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x}})}^{4}\\cdot x & = \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{{x}^{1/3}})}^{4}\\cdot x & = \\\\ {({x}^{-1/3})}^{4}\\cdot x & = \\\\ {x}^{(-1/3)\\cdot (4)}\\cdot x & = \\\\ {x}^{-4/3}\\cdot x & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule, adding the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-4/3}\\cdot x & = \\\\ {x}^{-4/3}\\cdot {x}^{1} & = \\\\ {x}^{-4/3+1} & = \\\\ {x}^{-4/3+3/3} & = \\\\ {x}^{-1/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $k=-\\dfrac{1}{3}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064298 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485583 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064299 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485532 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064299 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485567 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064299 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485581 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485491 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485494 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485519 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485553 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064300 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485592 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064301 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485550 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064302 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485517 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064302 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485563 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064303 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485536 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064303 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485539 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064304 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485504 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064304 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485511 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064328 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485546 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064449 1050 420.0 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064459 1050 420.0 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064680 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064680 1071 "${({x}^{3})}^{2}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064680 1071 "What is $(x^{3})^{2}$?" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186064680 1074 "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({x}^{3})}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{3\\cdot 2} & = \\\\ {x}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064686 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064686 1071 "Factor the expression $16a^{2} - 81$." 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186064686 1071 "Factor the expression $16{a}^{2}-81.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064686 1074 "First, we write the expression as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{a}^{2}-81 & =(4a)^{2}-{9}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4a)^{2}-{9}^{2} & =(4a+9)(4a-9)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064692 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064692 1071 "Express $4e^{2} - 16f^{2}$ as a product of two binomials." 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186064692 1071 "Express $4{e}^{2}-16{f}^{2}$ as a product of two binomials." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064692 1074 "We factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2}-16{f}^{2} & = \\\\ (2e)^{2}-(4f)^{2} & = \\\\ (2e-4f)(2e+4f) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064914 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064914 1071 "$\\frac{{a}^{5}}{{a}^{9}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186064914 1071 "What is $\\frac{a^{5}}{a^{9}}$?" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186064914 1074 "We subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}^{5}}{{a}^{9}} & = \\\\ {a}^{(5-9)} & = \\\\ {a}^{-4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we convert the negative exponent to a positive exponent by taking the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{-4} & =\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{5}}{{a}^{9}}=\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}.\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186065337 1064 "The **quotient rule for exponents** states that when we divide two exponents that have the same base, we take the difference of the exponents in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m}=a^{n-m}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a^{5}}{a^{2}} &=  \\\\\na^{(5 - 2)} &=  \\\\\n&= a^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see why the quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way.\n\nFor the two terms $a^5$ and $a^2$ we have\n\n$$\na^5 = a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\na^2 = a \\cdot a\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute these two expressions into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a^5}{a^2}\n$$\n\nwe get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{a^{5}}{a^2}&=\\ \\dfrac {a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {a \\cdot a}&=\\ \\dfrac {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a}} \\\\ &= a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\\\ &= a^3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above." 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186065337 1064 "The **quotient rule for exponents** states that when we divide two exponents that have the same base, we take the difference of the exponents in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m}=a^{n-m}\n$$\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}^{5}}{{a}^{2}} & = \\\\ {a}^{(5-2)} & = \\\\ {a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see why the quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way.\n\nFor the two terms $a^5$ and $a^2$ we have $a^5 = a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a$ and $a^2 = a \\cdot a.$ If we now substitute these two expressions into $\\dfrac{a^5}{a^2},$ we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{a^{5}}{a^2}&=\\\n\\dfrac {a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {a \\cdot a}&=\\\n\\dfrac {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} }&=\\\na \\cdot a \\cdot a &=\\\n a^3\n}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186065338 1064 "The **power rule for exponents** states that a power raised to another power is equal to the product of the two powers:\n\n$$\n\\left(a^m\\right)^n = a^{mn}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\left(5^2\\right)^3 \\\\ &= 5^{2 \\cdot 3} \\\\ &= 5^6. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see why the power rule for exponents is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\left(5^2\\right)^3 \\\\[5pt] &= \\underbrace{5^2\\cdot 5^2\\cdot 5^2}_{3\\text{times}} \\\\[5pt] &= (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\\\[5pt] &= \\underbrace{5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5}_{6\\text{times}} \\\\[5pt] &= 5^6 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power rule for exponents is just a shortcut for the process above." 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186065338 1064 "The **power rule for exponents** states that a power raised to another power is equal to the product of the two powers:\n\n$$\n\\left(a^m\\right)^n = a^{mn}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= \\\n\t\t\t\t5^{2 \\cdot 3} &= \\\n\t\t\t\t5^6.\n\t}\n$$\n\nTo see why the power rule for exponents is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\\underbrace{5^2\\cdot 5^2\\cdot 5^2}_{3\\text{ times}} &= \\[5pt]\n(5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\\underbrace{5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5}_{6\\text{ times}} &= \\[5pt]\n\t5^6\n}\n$$\n\nThe power rule for exponents is just a shortcut for the process above." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186065339 1064 "Suppose we want to remove the radical term in an expression like $\\sqrt{x^{3}}$. To do this, we first convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}}= \\left(x^{3}\\right)^{1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we can apply the power rule and multiply the fractional exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{3})^{1/2} &=  \\\\\nx^{(3) \\cdot (1/2)} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{3/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}} = x^{3/2}\n$$\n\nand in general,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{m/n}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186065339 1064 "Suppose we want to remove the radical term in an expression like $\\sqrt{x^{3}}.$ To do this, we first convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}}= \\left(x^{3}\\right)^{1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we can apply the power rule and multiply the fractional exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({x}^{3})}^{1/2} & = \\\\ {x}^{(3)\\cdot (1/2)} & = \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}} = x^{3/2},\n$$\n\nand in general,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{m/n}.\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186067012 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067013 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186067012 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067014 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186067012 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067015 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186067012 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067016 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186067012 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067017 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186067012 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484833 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186067012 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484969 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186067012 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485182 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186067012 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485194 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186067012 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485259 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186067012 1078 #edb/ref 17592186485182 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186068599 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x-1 \\\\  & 3{x}^{2}+x+2\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,6{x}^{3}-\\phantom{0}{x}^{2}+3x+1\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{6{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-3{x}^{2}-x+1 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,3{x}^{2}-x-2}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is ${\\color{blue}2x-1},$ and\n\n- the remainder is ${\\color{red}3}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186068599 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x-1 \\\\  & 3{x}^{2}+x+2\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,6{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+3x+1\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{6{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-3{x}^{2}-x+1 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,3{x}^{2}-x-2}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is ${\\color{blue}2x-1},$ and\n\n- the remainder is ${\\color{red}3}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186068600 1074 "Notice that the dividend is missing the $x^3$ term. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing term with a coefficient of zero, as follows:\n\n$$\n2x^4-7x^2-x+8 = 2x^4+{\\color{red}0}x^3-7x^2-x+8\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2{x}^{2}-5 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}-\\phantom{0}x+8\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-5{x}^{2}-x+8 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,5{x}^{2}+0x+5}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n- the quotient is ${2x^2 - 5},$ and\n\n- the remainder is ${-x+3}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^4 - 7x^2 - x +8}{x^2 - 1} \n= \n2x^2-5 + \\dfrac{-x+3}{x^2-1}.\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^4 - 7x^2 - x +8}{x^2 - 1} \n= \n2x^2-5 - \\dfrac{x-3}{x^2-1}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186068600 1074 "Notice that the dividend is missing the $x^3$ term. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing term with a coefficient of zero, as follows:\n\n$$\n2x^4-7x^2-x+8 = 2x^4+{\\color{red}0}x^3-7x^2-x+8\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2{x}^{2}-5 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}-x+8\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-5{x}^{2}-x+8 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,5{x}^{2}+0x+5}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n- the quotient is ${2x^2 - 5},$ and\n\n- the remainder is ${-x+3}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^4 - 7x^2 - x +8}{x^2 - 1} \n= \n2x^2-5 + \\dfrac{-x+3}{x^2-1}.\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^4 - 7x^2 - x +8}{x^2 - 1} \n= \n2x^2-5 - \\dfrac{x-3}{x^2-1}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186068761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186068761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485502 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186068761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485574 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186068762 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186068762 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485488 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186068762 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485490 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186068762 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485586 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186068817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485501 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186068817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485531 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186068817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485587 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186068960 1050 1260.0 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186069307 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186069307 1071 "$\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+6x-6}{{x}^{2}+x+9}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186069307 1071 "What is $\\frac{x^{3} - 2x^{2} + 6x - 6}{x^{2} + x + 9}$?" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186069307 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-3 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+x+9\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+6x-6\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+9x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-3{x}^{2}-3x-6 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,3{x}^{2}-3x-27}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n- the quotient is $x-3,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $21.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+6x-6}{{x}^{2}+x+9}=x-3+\\frac{21}{{x}^{2}+x+9}.\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186069974 1064 "The long division method allows us to divide a polynomial by another nonlinear polynomial.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{-4x^3+4x^2+x+4}{4x^2-1}\n$$\n\nHere, the divisor is the quadratic polynomial $4x^2-1$. \n\nAgain, we follow a step-by-step process.\n\n**Step 1**\n\nWe start by writing the dividend and the divisor as follows:\n\n![](images/30/30699e4c70a9787e299ddd72cf874be4d745d7765307118e244c7140900f9a8c.svg)\n\n**Step 2**\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}{-4x^3}})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{\\color{red}{-4x^3}}{{\\color{blue}{4x^2}}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{-x}}.$ We add this result to the quotient line:\n![](images/4d/4dc1f1597f96ce624bb5848694836fb7913119c5c5a771b8bc5cf2d5ce918a7f.svg)\n\nNext, we multiply the $\\color{#8b008b}-x$ we just found by the full divisor ${\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}-x}({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1)=-4x^3+x.$ We place this underneath the dividend, being sure to match the terms. Notice that we include a zero coefficient for the $x^2$ term.\n![](images/93/935c739a119cdb202ccf0ac919266888f79f2b002025a047df1350f85e8a0f14.svg)\n\nNow, we subtract $-4x^3+0x^2+x$ from $-4x^3+4x^2+x+4$ and get $4x^2+0x+4\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/3d/3d60d3f2969615aab7ee5d3fa32b95846558f6b2329fa530524b7ee5af53c8aa.svg)\n\n**Step 3**\n\nWe now repeat the process. The new dividend is ${\\color{red}{4x^2}}+0x+4.$\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}{4x^2}})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}{4x^2}}}{{\\color{blue}{4x^2}}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{1}}.$ We  add this result to the quotient:\n![](images/7b/7bc4e0d178e61af627a4e3c27e74f4dac447148144fcbd195bd064afe540c418.svg)\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{1}}$ we just found by the full divisor ${\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{1}}({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1)=4x^2-1.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n\n![](images/bf/bf35c387201a18236997a8e0687093fc51f9281b03374e864fd36176402dbbd8.svg)\n\nFinally, we subtract $4x^2+0x-1$ from $4x^2+0x+4$ to get ${\\color{red}{5}}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/68/68c9581aedc9c0bb2d061869483ddce11fe27579577d2a4d68c02e7c4cc9fc46.svg)\n\nWe're left with the constant $\\color{red}5$, which has a smaller degree than the divisor. So, the division is complete.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n- the quotient of the polynomial division is ${\\color{blue}{-x+1}},$ and\n\n- the remainder is ${\\color{red}{5}}.$\n\nOur complete result can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{-4x^3+4x^2+x+4}{4x^2-1}={\\color{blue}{-x+1}} + \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{4x^2-1}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186069974 1064 "The long division method allows us to divide a polynomial by another nonlinear polynomial.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{-4x^3+4x^2+x+4}{4x^2-1}\n$$\n\nHere, the divisor is the quadratic polynomial $4x^2-1$. \n\nAgain, we follow a step-by-step process.\n\n**Step 1**\n\nWe start by writing the dividend and the divisor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}{-4x^3}})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{\\color{red}{-4x^3}}{{\\color{blue}{4x^2}}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{-x}}.$ We add this result to the quotient line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the $\\color{#8b008b}-x$ we just found by the full divisor ${\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}-x}({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1)=-4x^3+x.$ We place this underneath the dividend, being sure to match the terms. Notice that we include a zero coefficient for the $x^2$ term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+x}_{―}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract $-4x^3+0x^2+x$ from $-4x^3+4x^2+x+4$ and get $4x^2+0x+4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}+0x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**\n\nWe now repeat the process. The new dividend is ${\\color{red}{4x^2}}+0x+4.$\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}{4x^2}})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}{4x^2}}}{{\\color{blue}{4x^2}}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{1}}.$ We  add this result to the quotient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x+1 \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}+0x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{1}}$ we just found by the full divisor ${\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{1}}({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1)=4x^2-1.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x+1 \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}+0x+4 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{2}+0x-1}_{―}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract $4x^2+0x-1$ from $4x^2+0x+4$ to get ${\\color{red}{5}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x+1 \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}+0x+4 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{2}+0x-1}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're left with the constant $\\color{red}5$, which has a smaller degree than the divisor. So, the division is complete.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n- the quotient of the polynomial division is ${\\color{blue}{-x+1}},$ and\n\n- the remainder is ${\\color{red}{5}}.$\n\nOur complete result can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{-4x^3+4x^2+x+4}{4x^2-1}={\\color{blue}{-x+1}} + \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{4x^2-1}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186073464 1071 "Simplify $8\\sqrt 3 - 5\\sqrt 3 + \\sqrt 5$." 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186073464 1071 "Simplify $8\\sqrt 3 - 5\\sqrt 3 + \\sqrt 5.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186073464 1074 "We can combine the two $\\sqrt{3}$ terms together, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8\\sqrt{3} - 5\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n(8 - 5)\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186073464 1074 "We can combine the two $\\sqrt{3}$ terms together, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{3}-5\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ (8-5)\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186073663 1071 "Simplify $2\\sqrt[3]{5} +5 \\sqrt{10} -3 \\sqrt[3]{40}$." 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186073663 1071 "Simplify $2\\sqrt[3]{5} +5 \\sqrt{10} -3 \\sqrt[3]{40}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186073663 1074 "First, we use the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt[3]{40} &= \\sqrt[3]{8 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt[3]{8} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{5} = 2\\sqrt[3]{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sqrt[3]{5} + 5\\sqrt{10} - 3\\sqrt[3]{40} &=  \\\\\n2\\sqrt[3]{5} + 5\\sqrt{10} - 3(2\\sqrt[3]{5}) &=  \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt[3]{5} + 5\\sqrt{10} - 6\\sqrt[3]{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sqrt[3]{5} + 5\\sqrt{10} - 6\\sqrt[3]{5} &=  \\\\\n5\\sqrt{10} + (2\\sqrt[3]{5} - 6\\sqrt[3]{5}) &=  \\\\\n5\\sqrt{10} + (2 - 6)\\sqrt[3]{5} &=  \\\\\n&= 5\\sqrt{10} - 4\\sqrt[3]{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186073663 1074 "First, we use the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{40} & =\\sqrt[3]{8\\cdot 5}=\\sqrt[3]{8}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{5}=2\\sqrt[3]{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt[3]{5}+5\\sqrt{10}-3\\sqrt[3]{40} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt[3]{5}+5\\sqrt{10}-3(2\\sqrt[3]{5}) & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt[3]{5}+5\\sqrt{10}-6\\sqrt[3]{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt[3]{5}+5\\sqrt{10}-6\\sqrt[3]{5} & = \\\\ 5\\sqrt{10}+(2\\sqrt[3]{5}-6\\sqrt[3]{5}) & = \\\\ 5\\sqrt{10}+(2-6)\\sqrt[3]{5} & = \\\\ 5\\sqrt{10}-4\\sqrt[3]{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186073664 1071 "Simplify the expression $3\\sqrt{80} -8\\sqrt{5}$." 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186073664 1071 "Simplify the expression $3\\sqrt{80} -8\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186073664 1074 "First, we use the product rule for radicals to simplify $\\sqrt{80}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{80} &= \\sqrt{16 \\cdot 5} = \\sqrt{16} \\cdot \\sqrt{5} = 4\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\sqrt{80} - 8\\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n3(4\\sqrt{5}) - 8\\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n&= 12\\sqrt{5} - 8\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt{5}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12\\sqrt{5} - 8\\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n(12 - 8)\\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n&= 4\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186073664 1074 "First, we use the product rule for radicals to simplify $\\sqrt{80},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{80} & =\\sqrt{16\\cdot 5}=\\sqrt{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=4\\sqrt{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{80}-8\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 3(4\\sqrt{5})-8\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 12\\sqrt{5}-8\\sqrt{5}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt{5}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\sqrt{5}-8\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ (12-8)\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 4\\sqrt{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186073698 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485593 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186073788 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485534 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186073788 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485537 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186073789 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485544 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186073789 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485566 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186073806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485495 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186073806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485554 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186073826 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485568 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186073842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485523 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186073921 1050 420.0 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186074948 1064 "When dealing with sums of square roots, we can combine any terms with the same number under the square root symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom 0}$.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt 5 + 4\\sqrt 5\n$$\n\nRemember that $2\\sqrt 5$ and $4\\sqrt 5$ mean $2\\cdot\\sqrt 5$ and $4\\cdot\\sqrt 5$, respectively.\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sqrt{5} + 4\\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n(2 + 4)\\sqrt{5} &=  \\\\\n&= 6\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sqrt{7} - \\sqrt{7} &=  \\\\\n2\\sqrt{7} - 1 \\cdot \\sqrt{7} &=  \\\\\n(2 - 1)\\sqrt{7} &=  \\\\\n1 \\cdot \\sqrt{7} &=  \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we *cannot* combine terms when the numbers under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt 3 + \\sqrt 5\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt 3$ and $\\sqrt 5$." 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186074948 1064 "When dealing with sums of square roots, we can combine any terms with the same number under the square root symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom 0}.$\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt 5 + 4\\sqrt 5\n$$\n\nRemember that $2\\sqrt 5$ and $4\\sqrt 5$ mean $2\\cdot\\sqrt 5$ and $4\\cdot\\sqrt 5,$ respectively. \n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}+4\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ (2+4)\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{7}-1\\cdot \\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ (2-1)\\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ 1\\cdot \\sqrt{7} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{7}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHowever, we *cannot* combine terms when the numbers under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt 3 + \\sqrt 5\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt 3$ and $\\sqrt 5.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186075012 1064 "Recall that the product rule for radicals states that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a\\cdot b} = \\sqrt{a}\\cdot \\sqrt{b}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are nonnegative numbers.\n\nWe can use the product rule for radicals to simplify some expressions. Let's see an example." 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186075012 1064 "Recall that the product rule for radicals states that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a\\cdot b} = \\sqrt{a}\\cdot \\sqrt{b}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are nonnegative numbers. \n\nWe can use the product rule for radicals to simplify some expressions. Let's see an example." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186075013 1064 "We can simplify cube root expressions in the same way as with square roots.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt[3]2 - 6\\sqrt[3] 2\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\sqrt[3]{2} - 6\\sqrt[3]{2} &=  \\\\\n(4 - 6)\\sqrt[3]{2} &=  \\\\\n&= - 2\\sqrt[3]{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs with square roots, we *cannot* combine terms when the numbers under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n3\\sqrt[3] 2 + \\sqrt[3] 5\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt[3] 2$ and $\\sqrt[3] 5$.\n\nFinally, it is not possible to combine square root and cube root terms -- not even if the numbers under the radical symbol are the same! For example, the expression\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt 2 + \\sqrt[3] 2\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt 2$ and $\\sqrt[3] 2$." 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186075013 1064 "We can simplify cube root expressions in the same way as with square roots.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt[3]2 - 6\\sqrt[3] 2\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt[3]{2}-6\\sqrt[3]{2} & = \\\\ (4-6)\\sqrt[3]{2} & = \\\\ -2\\sqrt[3]{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs with square roots, we *cannot* combine terms when the numbers under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n3\\sqrt[3] 2 + \\sqrt[3] 5\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt[3] 2$ and $\\sqrt[3] 5.$\n\nFinally, it is not possible to combine square root and cube root terms -- not even if the numbers under the radical symbol are the same! For example, the expression\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt 2 + \\sqrt[3] 2\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt 2$ and $\\sqrt[3] 2.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186075901 1078 #edb/ref 17592186075904 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186078532 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485499 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186078532 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485579 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186078853 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186078853 1071 "Evaluate the polynomial $-2x^{3} - 3$ at $x = 2$." 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186078853 1071 "Evaluate the polynomial $-2{x}^{3}-3$ at $x=2.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186078853 1074 "To evaluate, we substitute $x=2$ into the polynomial and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{3}-3 & = \\\\ -2(2)^{3}-3 & = \\\\ -2(8)-3 & = \\\\ -19\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186100233 1075 #edb/ref 17592186100234 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186100233 1075 #edb/ref 17592186100236 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186100233 1075 #edb/ref 17592186100237 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186100233 1075 #edb/ref 1759218610023