{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186101011 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485576 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186101011 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485591 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186101012 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485522 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186101013 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485508 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186101013 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485513 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186101014 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485492 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186101014 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485521 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186101014 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485528 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186101014 1038 #edb/ref 17592186485584 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186101075 1050 420.0 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186101512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186101512 1071 "${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}÷{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186101512 1071 "What is $(\\frac{1}{2})^{5} \\div (\\frac{1}{2})^{2}$?" 13194139535245 17592186045417 false] [17592186101512 1074 "To divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}÷{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5-2}={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186102023 1064 "To divide powers of the same base, we simply *subtract* the exponents. This is known as the **quotient rule for exponents**. For instance, in the expression\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\div {\\color{blue}3}^2\n$$\n\nboth powers have the same base $({\\color{blue}3})$. Therefore, we can simplify this expression by subtracting the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\div {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{5-2} = {\\color{blue}3}^3\n$$\n\n**Note:** The quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to the following process:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^{5} \\div 3^{2} &=  