4 #edb/ref 17592186485632 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485484 1084 #edb/ref 17592186485635 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485484 1085 "A school is holding a bake sale. All of the cakes and pies at the sale are of the same size." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485484 1086 #uuid "8ce01573-84d9-5c59-8210-9379d68685c7" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485484 1087 #edb/ref 17592186485635 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485433 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485485 1069 "q-105983" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485485 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485485 1071 "Given that $P(K|L)=0.25,$ $P(K)=0.75,$ and $P(L)=0.3,$ compute $P(L|K).$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485485 1072 #uuid "04948a98-4203-56b1-a1c8-9d3794661fc0" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485485 1074 "Using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(L|K) & =\\frac{P(K|L)P(L)}{P(K)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.25)(0.3)}{0.75} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.075}{0.75} \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485401 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485486 1069 "q-105586" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485486 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485486 1071 "What is the remainder when $3{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-6x+2$ is divided by $3{x}^{2}-2x+1?$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485486 1072 #uuid "055a4a11-a8c1-52ea-9a96-5ba018fca1a6" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485486 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x+3 \\\\  & 3{x}^{2}-2x+1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,3{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-6x+2\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,9{x}^{2}-7x+2 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{9{x}^{2}-6x+3}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is $x+3,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $-x-1.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485375 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485487 1069 "q-114524" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485487 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485487 1071 "$\\frac{{x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+2x+1}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485487 1072 #uuid "081856a1-5ee4-5d5e-9555-d794c0719197" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485487 1074 "Notice that the dividend is missing the ${x}^{3}$ term. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing term with a coefficient of zero, as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+2x+1={x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+2x+1\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+2x\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & {x}^{2}-2x+1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}+0{x}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+2x+1\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+2x+1 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+2x+0}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nAs a result, \n\n- the quotient is ${x}^{2}+2x,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+2x+1}{{x}^{2}-2x+1} & ={x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-2x+1} \\\\ .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485432 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485488 1069 "q-324442" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485488 1071 "What is the remainder of $-2{x}^{3}-8$ divided by $-{x}^{2}+2x-3?$\n\nThe remainder is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485488 1072 #uuid "09911eb6-f7c1-5633-957d-b9003c3e05af" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485488 1074 "Notice that the dividend is missing the ${x}^{2}$ and $x$ terms. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing terms with zero coefficients, as follows:\n\n$$\n-2{x}^{3}-8=-2{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+0x-8\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x+4 \\\\  & -{x}^{2}+2x-3\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-2{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+0x-\\phantom{0}8\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-6x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-4{x}^{2}+6x-8 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-4{x}^{2}+8x-12}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,2x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is $2x+4,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $-2x+4.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485450 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485458 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485489 1069 "q-133807" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485489 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485489 1071 "After Ellen takes her bite of Darryl's cherry pie, Darryl's remaining cherry pie is{{field-1}}\nthan Darryl's remaining apple pie because  $1\\frac{2}{7}$\n{{field-2}} $\\frac{4}{3}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485489 1072 #uuid "0a57b3c9-03a4-52de-a46c-64a4643241ad" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485489 1074 "Darryl has $1\\frac{2}{7}$ cherry pies and $\\frac{4}{3}$ apple pies remaining.\n\nWe cannot compare the fractions directly as they don't have a common denominator.\n\nWe can make a common denominator of $7\\times 3=21.$\n\nTo put $1\\frac{2}{7}$ over a denominator of $21,$ we first convert to an improper fraction and then multiply the numerator and denominator by $3:$\n\n$$\n1\\frac{2}{7}=\\frac{9}{7}=\\frac{9\\times 3}{7\\times 3}=\\frac{27}{21}\n$$\n\nTo put $\\frac{4}{3}$ over a denominator of $21,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $7:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{4\\times 7}{3\\times 7}=\\frac{28}{21}\n$$\n\nWe can now compare the fractions $\\frac{27}{21}$ and $\\frac{28}{21}$ by looking at the numerators only.\n\nSince $27<28,$ the correct statement must be:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{21}<\\frac{28}{21}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is\n\n> \"After Ellen takes her bite of Darryl's cherry pie, Darryl's remaining cherry pie is $<$ than Darryl's remaining apple pie because $1\\frac{2}{7}<\\frac{4}{3}.$\"" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485490 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485468 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485490 1069 "q-325144" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485490 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485490 1071 "What is the remainder of $-4{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}-9$ divided by ${x}^{2}-2x+3?$\n\nThe remainder is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485490 1072 #uuid "0aed3cc1-c2b0-58fe-afde-dbd43b0a8ab9" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485490 1074 "Notice that the dividend is missing the ${x}^{3}$ and $x$ terms. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing terms with zero coefficients, as follows:\n\n$$\n-4{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}-9=-4{x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+0x-9\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-4{x}^{2}-8x-5 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-2x+3\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-4{x}^{4}+0{x}^{3}-\\phantom{00}{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}0x-\\phantom{0}9\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,-4{x}^{4}+8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-8{x}^{3}+11{x}^{2}+0x-9 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,8{x}^{3}+16{x}^{2}-24x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-5{x}^{2}+24x-9 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-5{x}^{2}+10x-15}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,14x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is $-4{x}^{2}-8x-5,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $14x+6.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485392 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485491 1069 "q-72673" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485491 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485491 1071 "Simplify the  expression\n${u}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{{u}^{4}}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485491 1072 #uuid "0c16d02f-29ad-5f66-b01c-bdf6dca8d018" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485491 1074 "First we apply the quotient rule, subtracting exponents in the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{u}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{{u}^{4}} & = \\\\ {u}^{5}\\cdot {u}^{2-4} & = \\\\ {u}^{5}\\cdot {u}^{-2} & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we apply the product rule, adding exponents in the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{u}^{5}\\cdot {u}^{-2} & = \\\\ {u}^{5+(-2)} & = \\\\ {u}^{5-2} & = \\\\ {u}^{3} & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{u}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{{u}^{4}}={u}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485435 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485492 1069 "q-100366" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485492 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485492 1071 "$\\frac{{7}^{-5}}{{7}^{3}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485492 1072 #uuid "0c28763f-6ec1-59b6-a61c-8e135c29d4f7" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485492 1074 "To divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{7}^{-5}}{{7}^{3}}={7}^{(-5)-3}={7}^{-8}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485474 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485493 1069 "q-68977" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485493 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485493 1071 "$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{7}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485493 1072 #uuid "0ded43df-0d6f-557a-8481-c4307edc8e48" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485493 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{7}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{7}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{7}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{7}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{7} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the product rule to simplify the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 7}}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485386 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485494 1069 "q-72677" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485494 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485494 1071 "Simplify ${a}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{{a}^{-3}}{{a}^{2}}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485494 1072 #uuid "0e69e5e6-7016-5a90-88d1-935042e944fb" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485494 1074 "First we apply the quotient rule, subtracting exponents in the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{{a}^{-3}}{{a}^{2}} & = \\\\ {a}^{5}\\cdot {a}^{(-3-2)} & = \\\\ {a}^{5}\\cdot {a}^{-5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we apply the product rule, adding exponents in the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{a}^{5}\\cdot {a}^{-5} & = \\\\ {a}^{5+(-5)} & = \\\\ {a}^{5-5} & = \\\\ {a}^{0} & = \\\\ 1 & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{a}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{{a}^{-3}}{{a}^{2}}=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485420 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485495 1069 "q-82091" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485495 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485495 1071 "Simplify $2\\sqrt{2}+4\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485495 1072 #uuid "0ea8ccf7-5897-5ab4-80b3-c1985abc7d80" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485495 1074 "We can combine the two $\\sqrt{2}$ terms together, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}+4\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ (2+4)\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ 6\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485496 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485428 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485496 1069 "q-212962" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485496 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485496 1071 "$\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{27}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485496 1072 #uuid "0f188433-9891-58cb-8279-ce3757720a45" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485496 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{27}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{27}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{27}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{27}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{27}}{\\sqrt{27}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{27}}{(\\sqrt{27})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{27}}{27} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we notice that $27$ has a perfect square factor $(9).$ Therefore, we can rewrite $\\sqrt{27}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{27} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9\\cdot 3} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify our original fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{27}}{27} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}}{27} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{27} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{9} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5\\cdot 3}}{9} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485497 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485368 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485497 1069 "q-106499" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485497 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485497 1071 "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $90%$ of those infected and $9%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $14%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested negative is not infected?  Round your answer to four decimal places." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485497 1072 #uuid "10d543bf-463f-57f9-8fa9-461f96d806af" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485497 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP({D}^{′}|{T}^{′})=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′})}=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)+P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $90%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $9%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP({T}^{′}|D)=1-0.9=0.1,\\,P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})=1-0.09=0.91.\n$$\n\n- Since only $14%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.14\\,P({D}^{′})=1-0.14=0.86.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}) & =\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)+P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.91)(0.86)}{(0.1)(0.14)+(0.91)(0.86)} \\\\  & ≈0.9824\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded four decimal places." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485380 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485498 1069 "q-5793" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485498 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485498 1071 "Two identical boxes contain marbles. The first box contains two black and four blue marbles. The second box contains one black and five blue marbles. A box is selected at random, and one marble is chosen from the selected box, also at random. If the selected marble is black, what is the probability that it came from the first box?" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485498 1072 #uuid "111248ff-8060-5580-819b-222a636556c6" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485498 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that the first box is selected, and let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected marble is black. Then, we require $P(F|B),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem. \n\n$$\nP(F|B)=\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B|F)P(F)+P(B|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})}\n$$\n\nNote that ${F}^{′}$ is the event that the second box is selected.\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the first box contains two black and four blue marbles, and the second box contains one black and five blue marbles, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|F)=\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{1}{3},\\,P(B|{F}^{′})=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\n- Since the box is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=P({F}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B|F)P(F)+P(B|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{6})}{(\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{12})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485426 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485499 1069 "q-74463" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485499 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485499 1071 "Given the polynomial $g(x)={x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-4x,$ $g(1)=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485499 1072 #uuid "14cc1a32-5526-5826-8d11-3b9752e2a607" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485499 1074 "We substitute $x=1$ into the expression for $g(x)$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-4x \\\\ g(1) & ={1}^{4}+3\\cdot {1}^{3}+{1}^{2}-4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+3+1-4 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(1)=1.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485500 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485467 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485500 1069 "q-106226" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485500 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485500 1071 "A survey of Bulgarian citizens showed that $17%$ of all females and  $38%$ of all males smoke. $51%$ of the population are female. If a non-smoker from this population is selected at random, what is the probability of them being female? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485500 1072 #uuid "181eb68d-e7ae-553c-958d-610b9662b27f" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485500 1074 "Let $N$ be the event that a randomly selected person doesn't smoke, and let $F$ be the event that they are female. Then, we require $P(F|N),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(F|N)=\\frac{P(N|F)P(F)}{P(N)}=\\frac{P(N|F)P(F)}{P(N|F)P(F)+P(N|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since $17%$ of all females and  $38%$ of all males smoke, we have\n\n$$\nP(N|F)=1-0.17=0.83,\\,P(N|{F}^{′})=1-0.38=0.62.\n$$\n\n- Since $51%$ of the population are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.51,\\,P({F}^{′})=1-0.51=0.49.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|N) & =\\frac{P(N|F)P(F)}{P(N|F)P(F)+P(N|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.83)(0.51)}{(0.83)(0.51)+(0.62)(0.49)} \\\\  & ≈58%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485501 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485365 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485501 1069 "q-105619" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485501 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485501 1071 "What is the quotient of $2{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x+2$ divided by ${x}^{2}-x+3?$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485501 1072 #uuid "181fbf98-acb9-50e0-be18-f71c1745254f" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485501 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2{x}^{2}+1 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-x+3\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,2{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x+2\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}-x+2 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{2}-x+3}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is $2{x}^{2}+1,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $-1.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485394 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485445 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485502 1069 "q-322775" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485502 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485502 1071 "Express $\\frac{18{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-53x+32}{3{x}^{2}-5x+2}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{3{x}^{2}-5x+2},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the reminder of the division.$$\n\n| $18{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-53x+32$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3{x}^{2}-5x+2$ | __ |  |  | $3{x}^{2}-5x+2$ |" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485502 1072 #uuid "19e2fba4-263d-5d97-b42a-74a9677543ff" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485502 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6x+15 \\\\  & 3{x}^{2}-5x+2\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,18{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-53x+32\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{18{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+12x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,45{x}^{2}-65x+32 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,45{x}^{2}-75x+30}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,10x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n- the quotient is $6x+15,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $10x+2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{18{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-53x+32}{3{x}^{2}-5x+2}=6x+15+\\frac{10x+2}{3{x}^{2}-5x+2}.\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485402 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485503 1069 "q-127705" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485503 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485503 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨t\\sqrt{5},\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=3t,$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485503 1072 #uuid "1bd4e151-d470-5686-8d26-76a2f90fc9ab" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485503 1074 "First, since $s=3t,$ we have that $t=\\frac{s}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{3}) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{s\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parameterized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s\\sqrt{5}}{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}),\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\sqrt{5},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485479 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485504 1069 "q-23277" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485504 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485504 1071 "Given that  ${a}^{12}=t$ and ${a}^{4}=s$, what is the value of $\\frac{{t}^{3}}{s}?$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485504 1072 #uuid "1d4b1ea1-1846-5966-be56-ba39843c102c" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485504 1074 "First, we carry out the substitution\n\n$$\n\\frac{{t}^{3}}{s}=\\frac{{({a}^{12})}^{3}}{{a}^{4}}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule followed by the quotient rule \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{({a}^{12})}^{3}}{{a}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}^{12\\cdot 3}}{{a}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}^{36}}{{a}^{4}} & = \\\\ {a}^{36-4} & = \\\\ {a}^{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485399 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485505 1069 "q-177668" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485505 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a square matrix\n\nII. ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485505 1072 #uuid "203918fb-bff3-5640-9116-ee4c833a081b" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485505 1074 "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The number of rows in $A$ is not the same as the number of columns. So, it's not a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\cdot A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\ne I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not a square matrix and ${A}^{T}\\cdot A\\ne I,$ our matrix is **not** orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements.\"" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485473 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485506 1069 "q-177845" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485506 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485506 1072 #uuid "222e2ed5-e7e4-55fe-9d04-24be2fce81e6" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485506 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485507 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485373 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485507 1069 "q-177925" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485507 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485507 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485507 1072 #uuid "227d7c9d-e02b-5883-bb19-91ec1f4f8788" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485507 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-4)+(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485508 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485471 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485508 1069 "q-100346" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485508 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485508 1071 "$(-1.8)^{9}÷(-1.8)^{3}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485508 1072 #uuid "23b2c3db-685f-514d-a3c0-859f60e7e231" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485508 1074 "To divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n(-1.8)^{9}÷(-1.8)^{3}=(-1.8)^{9-3}=(-1.8)^{6}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485403 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485509 1069 "q-42560" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485509 1071 "In a particular school, $65%$ of students attend a literature course, and $45%$ of students attend a mathematics course. If $50%$ of mathematics students also attend a literature course, what is the probability that a randomly chosen literature student attends a mathematics course? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485509 1072 #uuid "242fce9f-3cc9-573a-8f6b-cabffe543cea" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485509 1074 "Let $L$ be the event that a randomly selected student attends a literature course, and let $M$ be the event that they attend a mathematics course. Then, we require $P(M|L),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|L)=\\frac{P(L|M)P(M)}{P(L)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(L|M)=0.5,$ since $50%$ of mathematics students also attend a literature course.\n\n- $P(M)=0.45,$ since $45%$ of students attend a mathematics course.\n\n- $P(L)=0.65,$ since $65%$ of students attend a literature course.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|L) & =\\frac{P(L|M)P(M)}{P(L)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.5)(0.45)}{0.65} \\\\  & ≈0.35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485422 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485510 1069 "q-106939" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485510 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*If $w$ and $v$ are vectors such that $x+w=v+x$ for some $x\\in V$, then $w=v.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =v+x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =v+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =v+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w & =v &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485510 1072 #uuid "2e41850a-28e4-5827-a5c3-9f3e4e6f3d17" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485510 1074 "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+w & =v+x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+w)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (w+x)+(-x) & =(v+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A1}\\,] \\\\ w+(x+(-x)) & =v+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =v+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =v &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485397 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485511 1069 "q-22798" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485511 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485511 1071 "If ${b}^{9}=x$ and ${b}^{2}=y$, what is the value of $\\frac{{x}^{3}}{y}?$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485511 1072 #uuid "2e9f1022-1926-5a16-afea-9527e06c53ee" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485511 1074 "First, we carry out the substitution\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}}{y}=\\frac{{({b}^{9})}^{3}}{{b}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply power rule followed by the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{({b}^{9})}^{3}}{{b}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{b}^{9\\cdot 3}}{{b}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{b}^{27}}{{b}^{2}} & = \\\\ {b}^{27-2} & = \\\\ {b}^{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485470 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485512 1069 "q-41706" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485512 1071 "Solve for $x$ in $Ax=b$ given that the matrix $A$ is orthogonal, and \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485512 1072 #uuid "340e44da-12e6-5ccb-a96a-bc97b2d0d915" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485512 1074 "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nHence, since $A$ is orthogonal, we have ${A}^{-1}={A}^{T}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485409 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485513 1069 "q-100343" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485513 1071 "$\\frac{{0.01}^{6}}{{0.01}^{8}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485513 1072 #uuid "34da0315-7401-50d5-a9e5-7e5606f6c11f" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485513 1074 "First, we write the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{0.01}^{6}}{{0.01}^{8}}={0.01}^{6}÷{0.01}^{8}\n$$\n\nTo divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0.01}^{6}÷{0.01}^{8} & ={0.01}^{6-8} \\\\  & ={0.01}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485366 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485514 1069 "q-84784" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485514 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n\nI. If $A=PB{P}^{-1}$ then $|det(A)|=|det(B)|$\n\nII. $PQ$ is always orthogonal\n\nIII. $2P+Q$ is **not** always orthogonal" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485514 1072 #uuid "35c37699-f2ab-5493-8b62-dd508d98be99" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485514 1074 "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing $|det(A)|,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|det(PB{P}^{-1})| \\\\  & =|det(PB{P}^{T})| \\\\  & =|det(P)|\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot |det({P}^{T})| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|det(B)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(PQ)\\cdot (PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ\\cdot (PQ)^{T} & =(PQ)\\cdot {Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{P}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. As a counterexample, consider the following sum involving two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example, the first column is not normalized.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485462 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485515 1069 "q-51806" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485515 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${(4wz)}^{2}?$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485515 1072 #uuid "36aeac2d-5deb-59f2-8d1b-a76ca9d4f792" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485515 1074 "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(4wz)}^{2} & = \\\\ {4}^{2}{w}^{2}{z}^{2} & = \\\\ 16{w}^{2}{z}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485371 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485516 1069 "q-84245" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485516 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{8} \\\\ \\sqrt{8} & 4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind $|\\text{det}(PQ)|$ given that $P$ is an orthogonal matrix." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485516 1072 #uuid "36bba55d-9122-58f3-aa1e-57a8674f5e02" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485516 1074 "We use the following facts: \n\n- The determinant of a product is equal to the product of the determinants.\n\n- The determinant of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is equal to $\\pm 1.$\n\nApplying these ideas, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\text{det}(PQ)| & =|\\text{det}(P)\\cdot \\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|(\\pm 1)\\cdot \\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|\\pm 1|\\cdot |\\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|\\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 4\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{8}\\cdot \\sqrt{8}| \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485417 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485517 1069 "q-247" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485517 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485517 1071 "If ${({y}^{4})}^{-2}={y}^{k},$ then $k=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485517 1072 #uuid "3a39a434-9b02-5a2a-9dbe-af926f01aaf3" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485517 1074 "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({y}^{4})}^{-2} & = \\\\ {y}^{4\\cdot (-2)} & = \\\\ {y}^{-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $k=-8.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485393 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485518 1069 "q-40515" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485518 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n\nI. $U=\\{Q([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{Q([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}])\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{Q([\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}])\\}$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485518 1072 #uuid "3a3b6415-46fa-5ac6-8f1e-cce0985c5f71" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485518 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nLet's denote the elements of $U,W,$ and $V$ respectively as \n\n$\\,$ $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\},\\,$ $\\{Q({v}_{1}),Q({v}_{2}),Q({v}_{3})\\},\\,$ and $\\,\\{Q({w}_{1}),Q({w}_{2})\\}.$\n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2})=0,\\,Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0,\\,Q({v}_{2})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0.$\n\n- $Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2}) & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=20-3=17\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not and orthogonal set.\n\n- For the Set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]=3-4+1=0,\\,✓ \\\\ Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=2+8+18=28\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2}) & ={w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]=-15+15=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$W$ only\"." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485419 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485519 1069 "q-72679" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485519 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485519 1071 "Simplify the  expression\n$\\frac{n}{{n}^{4}}\\cdot {n}^{8}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485519 1072 #uuid "3be04767-4c26-5347-b891-6186a74ead6b" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485519 1074 "First we apply the quotient rule, subtracting exponents in the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{n}{{n}^{4}}\\cdot {n}^{8} & = \\\\ {n}^{1-4}\\cdot {n}^{8} & = \\\\ {n}^{-3}\\cdot {n}^{8} & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we apply the product rule, adding exponents in the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{n}^{-3}\\cdot {n}^{8} & = \\\\ {n}^{-3+8} & = \\\\ {n}^{5} & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{n}{{n}^{4}}\\cdot {n}^{8}={n}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485411 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485520 1069 "q-68832" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485520 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485520 1071 "$\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{6}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485520 1072 #uuid "3e56e0ed-1ba2-5a25-8811-643841869c5d" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485520 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{6}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{6}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{6}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485369 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485521 1069 "q-68123" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485521 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485521 1071 "$\\frac{{5}^{4}}{{5}^{6}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485521 1072 #uuid "3f475ee2-0efc-5b85-a0eb-323e7d9761d6" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485521 1074 "To divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{5}^{4}}{{5}^{6}}={5}^{4-6}={5}^{-2}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate ${5}^{-2},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $(5)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{5}^{-2}={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{5}\\times \\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{1}{25}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485370 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485522 1069 "q-68125" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485522 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485522 1071 "${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{7}÷{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485522 1072 #uuid "4082ce79-1f09-55c7-aa8e-8877c477aa68" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485522 1074 "To divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{7}÷{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{7-2}={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485523 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485434 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485523 1069 "q-133804" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485523 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485523 1071 "If a total of $9$ people made all the cakes for the sale, and each person made the same number of cakes, then they each made{{field-1}}\ncakes." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485523 1072 #uuid "43bd5eed-55d7-5512-b491-efa9bc5b652f" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485523 1074 "We know that there are $216$ cakes in total. So, each person made $216÷9$ cakes.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{2\\,\\phantom{|}1\\,\\phantom{|}6}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNotice that $2$ (the number of hundreds in $216$) is less than the divisor $9.$ Therefore, we should consider both the hundreds and tens:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{2\\,\\phantom{|}1\\,\\phantom{|}6}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$↓$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Divide: $21÷9=2\\,\\text{R}\\,3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Write over tens: $2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Multiply: $9\\times 2=18$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Subtract: $21-18=3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Bring down: $6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{2\\,\\phantom{|}1\\,\\phantom{|}6}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\boxed{\\text{0}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Divide: $36÷9=4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Write over ones: $4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Multiply: $9\\times 4=36$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Subtract: $36-36=0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe get the remainder $0$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n216÷9=24.\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485524 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485469 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485524 1069 "q-177659" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485524 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485524 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a square matrix\n\nII. $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485524 1072 #uuid "46db878d-0e5d-50fb-979a-d499bbe2ee60" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485524 1074 "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{24}{25} \\\\ \\frac{24}{25} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\ne I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A\\cdot {A}^{T}\\ne I,$ our matrix is **not** orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485427 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485525 1069 "q-131031" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485525 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485525 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}-x-y$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,-2,3⟩,$ respectively." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485525 1072 #uuid "46ec3a18-4fb6-5ee1-a9e8-e76913b4fb34" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485525 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}-x-y$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,-2,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1,-2,3)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨-1,-2,3⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 12-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,-2,3⟩ \\\\ 12 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,-2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-1,-1,2z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-1,-1,2{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,-2,3⟩ \\\\ 12 & =⟨-1,-1,2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,-2,3⟩ \\\\ 12 & =1+2+6{c}_{3} \\\\ 6{c}_{3} & =9 \\\\ {c}_{3} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{(-1)} & =\\frac{y-0}{(-2)}=\\frac{z-0}{3} \\\\ -x & =-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=-t,\\,y=-2t,\\,z=3t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=-t,\\,{c}_{2}=-2t,\\,{c}_{3}=3t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation ${c}_{3}=\\frac{3}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3t & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-2(\\frac{1}{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(-\\frac{1}{2},-1,\\frac{3}{2}).$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485404 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485526 1069 "q-32628" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485526 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485526 1071 "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485526 1072 #uuid "485b91ef-f664-5968-befd-b6e37e67e294" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485526 1074 "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U.\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ C & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485421 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485527 1069 "q-213446" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485527 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485527 1071 "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485527 1072 #uuid "4b284fb0-af6c-5838-8a95-3a08ee9f35c3" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485527 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{7}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{7}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485387 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485528 1069 "q-185293" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485528 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485528 1071 "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, $\\frac{{2}^{-2}}{{2}^{2}}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485528 1072 #uuid "4c73aa99-debd-5794-8ba7-65ccf58c6b12" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485528 1074 "To divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{2}^{-2}}{{2}^{2}}={2}^{-2-2}={2}^{-4}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate ${2}^{-4},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $(2)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{2}^{-4}={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}=\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{16}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485529 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485412 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485529 1069 "q-140564" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485529 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485529 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q?$\n\nI. ${Q}^{2}$ is orthogonal\n\nII. $Q$ is a diagonal matrix\n\nIII. $Q-I$ is invertible" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485529 1072 #uuid "4cd1f663-1b26-5239-80df-41dfbaf9dd53" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485529 1074 "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the product ${Q}^{2}\\cdot ({Q}^{2})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{2}\\cdot ({Q}^{2})^{T} & =(Q\\cdot Q)\\cdot (Q\\cdot Q)^{T} \\\\  & =(Q\\cdot Q)\\cdot ({Q}^{T}\\cdot {Q}^{T}) \\\\  & =Q\\cdot (Q\\cdot {Q}^{T})\\cdot {Q}^{T} \\\\  & =Q(I){Q}^{T} \\\\  & =Q{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix:\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n$Q$ is an orthogonal matrix, but it is not a diagonal matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, let $Q=I$ be the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix. Then, \n\n$$\nQ-I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe $2\\times 2$  zero matrix, which is not an invertible matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485530 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485480 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485530 1069 "q-29261" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485530 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485530 1071 "$\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485530 1072 #uuid "4efb3768-338e-56ca-8759-51144eadd947" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485530 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{5}$ from the denominator, we need to multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{5},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{5} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485451 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485466 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485531 1069 "q-270162" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485531 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485531 1071 "Express $\\frac{-6{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-10}{2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+4x-2}$ in the form $q(x)+\\frac{r(x)}{2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+4x-2},$ where $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the reminder of the division.$$\n\n| $-6{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-10$ | __ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+4x-2$ | __ |  |  | $2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+4x-2$ |" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485531 1072 #uuid "50f23ba0-92ed-55f7-8001-c1ea7b79fdae" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485531 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-3x-4 \\\\  & 2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+4x-2\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-6{x}^{4}+\\phantom{0}{x}^{3}-\\phantom{0}2{x}^{2}+\\phantom{00}x-10\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,-\\,6{x}^{4}+9{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+6x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-8{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-5x-10 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,-\\,8{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}-16x+8}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,2{x}^{2}+11x-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n- the quotient is $-3x-4,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $-2{x}^{2}+11x-18.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-6{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-10}{2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+4x-2} & =-3x-4+\\frac{(-2{x}^{2}+11x-18)}{2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+4x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485405 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485532 1069 "q-72667" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485532 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{{u}^{-7}}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485532 1072 #uuid "51b4f2a2-d5b5-571a-9fa7-6251a05de673" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485532 1074 "We subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{{u}^{-7}} & = \\\\ {u}^{-4-(-7)} & = \\\\ {u}^{-4+7} & = \\\\ {u}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485383 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485533 1069 "q-42558" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485533 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485533 1071 "In a particular school, $70%$ of students attend a history course, and $40%$ of students attend an art course. If $50%$ of art students also attend a history course, what is the probability that a randomly chosen history student also attends an art course? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485533 1072 #uuid "56a74bdd-02d9-5cb6-a536-9db069af271c" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485533 1074 "Let $H$ be the event that a randomly selected student attends a history course, and let $A$ be the event that they attend an art course. Then, we require $P(A|H),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(A|H)=\\frac{P(H|A)P(A)}{P(H)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(H|A)=0.5,$ since $50%$ of art students attend a history course.\n\n- $P(A)=0.4,$ since $40%$ of students attend an art course.\n\n- $P(H)=0.7,$ since $70%$ of students attend a history course.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|H) & =\\frac{P(H|A)P(A)}{P(H)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.5)(0.4)}{0.7} \\\\  & ≈0.29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485456 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485534 1069 "q-134564" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485534 1071 "Simplify $4\\sqrt[3]{500}+\\sqrt[3]{4}-\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485534 1072 #uuid "589b43ec-34cc-5c99-9404-4a02a5e69293" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485534 1074 "First, we use the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{500} & =\\sqrt[3]{125\\cdot 4}=\\sqrt[3]{125}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{4}=5\\sqrt[3]{4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt[3]{500}+\\sqrt[3]{4}-\\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ 4(5\\sqrt[3]{4})+\\sqrt[3]{4}-\\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ 20\\sqrt[3]{4}+\\sqrt[3]{4}-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt[3]{4}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20\\sqrt[3]{4}+\\sqrt[3]{4}-\\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ (20\\sqrt[3]{4}+\\sqrt[3]{4})-\\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ (20+1)\\sqrt[3]{4}-\\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ 21\\sqrt[3]{4}-\\sqrt{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485454 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485535 1069 "q-130275" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485535 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485535 1071 "Find $AB+CB$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 9 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{7} & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -9 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485535 1072 #uuid "59d9ac6b-9ad1-5629-b5da-d053470d3899" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485535 1074 "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB+CB=(A+C)B.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+C & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 9 & 9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -9 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB+CB & =(A+C)B \\\\  & =5{I}_{3}B \\\\  & =5B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}40 & 5 & 5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 15 & 25 & 5 \\\\ -10\\sqrt{7} & 5 & 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485406 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485536 1069 "q-104647" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485536 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485536 1071 "The equivalent radical expression for ${({(\\frac{2}{u})}^{1/4})}^{7/3}$ is" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485536 1072 #uuid "5af0acf2-3a19-5539-aae2-8a4b5d437d31" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485536 1074 "First, we apply the power rule and then we express the resulting fractional power as a radical: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({(\\frac{2}{u})}^{1/4})}^{7/3} & = \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{u})}^{(1/4)(7/3)} & = \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{u})}^{7/12} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[12]{{(\\frac{2}{u})}^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485407 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485537 1069 "q-134567" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485537 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{7}+5\\sqrt[3]{162}-\\sqrt[3]{6}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485537 1072 #uuid "5ea12d57-149c-5e75-92c8-881bf2f1c4e9" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485537 1074 "First, we use the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{162} & =\\sqrt[3]{27\\cdot 6}=\\sqrt[3]{27}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{6}=3\\sqrt[3]{6} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}+5\\sqrt[3]{162}-\\sqrt[3]{6} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{7}+5(3\\sqrt[3]{6})-\\sqrt[3]{6}= \\\\ \\sqrt{7}+15\\sqrt[3]{6}-\\sqrt[3]{6}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt[3]{6}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7}+15\\sqrt[3]{6}-\\sqrt[3]{6}= \\\\ \\sqrt{7}+(15\\sqrt[3]{6}-\\sqrt[3]{6}) & = \\\\ \\sqrt{7}+(15-1)\\sqrt[3]{6} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{7}+14\\sqrt[3]{6} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485398 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485441 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485443 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485538 1069 "q-319197" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485538 1071 "Calculate $AC+BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n|  |  | $2$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AC+BC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} | $4$ | $2$ |  |\n|  |  | $5$ | {{field-3}} | $3$ |  |" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485538 1072 #uuid "5fdc95ea-e1eb-5331-974c-7dbe555559e4" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485538 1074 "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAC+BC=(A+B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC+BC & =(A+B)C \\\\  & ={I}_{3}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485437 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485539 1069 "q-104646" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485539 1071 "The equivalent radical expression for ${({r}^{3/2})}^{1/4}$ is" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485539 1072 #uuid "620bd9a5-9e55-517b-86cd-e6524b612924" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485539 1074 "First, we apply the power rule and then we express the resulting fractional power as a radical: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({r}^{3/2})}^{1/4} & = \\\\ {r}^{(3/2)(1/4)} & = \\\\ {r}^{3/8} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[8]{{r}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485476 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485540 1069 "q-72563" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485540 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485540 1071 "${(5w{z}^{-5})}^{2}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485540 1072 #uuid "6a85d6f0-241d-535a-97b2-3383eef73354" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485540 1074 "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(5w{z}^{-5})}^{2} & = \\\\ {5}^{2}\\cdot {w}^{2}\\cdot {({z}^{-5})}^{2} & = \\\\ {5}^{2}\\cdot {w}^{2}\\cdot {z}^{-5\\cdot 2} & = \\\\ 25\\cdot {w}^{2}\\cdot {z}^{-10} & = \\\\ \\frac{25{w}^{2}}{{z}^{10}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485381 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485541 1069 "q-212913" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485541 1071 "$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{3}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485541 1072 #uuid "70917dc8-1a43-5c37-8280-8659e1c78b60" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485541 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{3}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{3}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{3\\cdot (\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{3\\cdot 3} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the product rule to simplify the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{9} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 3}}{9} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{9} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485414 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485542 1069 "q-129738" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485542 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485542 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{4}{3}(1+t)^{3/2},\\,t,\\,\\frac{4}{3}(1-t)^{3/2}⟩,$ $-1\\le t\\le 1,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=3t,$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485542 1072 #uuid "72798764-caa8-5165-8cde-e9f5d6b201ad" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485542 1074 "First, since $s=3t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{3}) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{3}{(1+\\frac{s}{3})}^{3/2},\\,\\frac{s}{3},\\,\\frac{4}{3}{(1-\\frac{s}{3})}^{3/2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parameterized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{4}{3}{(1+\\frac{s}{3})}^{3/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{4}{3}{(1+\\frac{s}{3})}^{3/2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{s}{3}},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{1-\\frac{s}{3}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\sqrt{3+s},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\sqrt{3-s}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485374 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485543 1069 "q-75612" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485543 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485543 1071 "Find $AC+BC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485543 1072 #uuid "72e0b567-7c07-513e-b4b8-2088d6f698dd" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485543 1074 "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAC+BC=(A+B)C.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A+B$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 6 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC+BC & =(A+B)C \\\\  & =3{I}_{3}C \\\\  & =3C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3\\sqrt{2} & 3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 6 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 6 \\\\ 9 & 6 & 3\\sqrt{5}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485464 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485544 1069 "q-134543" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485544 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485544 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{125}-2\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485544 1072 #uuid "72ebf39d-3bb6-58bc-806b-137a4fead9d6" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485544 1074 "First, we use the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{125} & =\\sqrt{25\\cdot 5}=\\sqrt{25}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=5\\sqrt{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{125}-2\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 5\\sqrt{5}-2\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt{5}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{5}-2\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ (5\\sqrt{5}+3\\sqrt{5})-2\\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ (5+3)\\sqrt{5}-2\\sqrt{3} & = \\\\ 8\\sqrt{5}-2\\sqrt{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485396 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485545 1069 "q-106497" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485545 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485545 1071 "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $91%$ of those infected and $4%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $15%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested positive is not infected? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485545 1072 #uuid "7540b1df-730f-5d2d-b7a8-be03db2a980d" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485545 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P({D}^{′}|T),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP({D}^{′}|T)=\\frac{P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P(T)}=\\frac{P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})+P(T|D)P(D)}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $91%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $4%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(T|D)=0.91,\\,P(T|{D}^{′})=0.04.\n$$\n\n- Since only $15%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.15,\\,P({D}^{′})=1-0.15=0.85.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({D}^{′}|T) & =\\frac{P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})+P(T|D)P(D)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.04)(0.85)}{(0.04)(0.85)+(0.91)(0.15)} \\\\  & ≈20%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485423 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485546 1069 "q-44102" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485546 1071 "$81-{x}^{2}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485546 1072 #uuid "7a010f5a-cc94-5c4d-81d2-4c21165ae9ab" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485546 1074 "First, we write the expression as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n81-{x}^{2}={9}^{2}-{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{9}^{2}-{x}^{2}=(9-x)(9+x)\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485453 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485547 1069 "q-133808" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485547 1071 "Darryl gives $\\frac{2}{5}$ of his apple pies to his daughter. What fraction of a whole pie does Darryl's daughter have?\n\n*Remember that Darryl bought $\\frac{4}{3}$ of an apple pie.*\n\nDarryl's daughter has\n{{field-1}}\nof a pie." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485547 1072 #uuid "7d63aa21-54c3-5793-bdf0-1f7c092e61d0" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485547 1074 "We know that Darryl has $\\frac{4}{3}$ of an apple pie.\n\nTo calculate the fraction of a whole pie that Darryl's daughter has, we need to multiply $\\frac{2}{5}$ by $\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{5}\\times \\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{2\\times 4}{5\\times 3}=\\frac{8}{15}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Darry's daughter have $\\frac{8}{15}$ of a pie." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485548 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485477 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485548 1069 "q-133803" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485548 1071 "At the bake sale, there are $12$ tables with $18$ cakes at each table. Therefore, there are{{field-1}}\ncakes in total." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485548 1072 #uuid "7dffbb18-69db-5548-9e20-8e99e6ab3d7c" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485548 1074 "To calculate the total number of cakes, we need to multiply $12$ by $18.$\n\nSo, we write it this way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  &  &  & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ \\boxed{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}12\\times 8=96\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}12\\times 10=120\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{1}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}96+120=216\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12\\times 18=216.$\n\nTherefore, there are $216$ cakes in total." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485436 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485549 1069 "q-22334" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485549 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485549 1071 "Simplify $-(-4b)^{3}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485549 1072 #uuid "7f18a486-2b6b-5f9e-8e38-324212260786" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485549 1074 "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(-4b)^{3} & = \\\\ -((-4)^{3}\\cdot {b}^{3}) & = \\\\ -(-64{b}^{3}) & = \\\\ 64{b}^{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485382 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485550 1069 "q-670" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485550 1071 "Simplify ${({2}^{2})}^{3}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485550 1072 #uuid "838d378f-b0d1-5666-9116-5d6a99c6b6ef" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485550 1074 "First, we multiply the exponents and then we calculate the resulting power of \n$2.$\n:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({2}^{2})}^{3} & = \\\\ {2}^{(2\\cdot 3)} & = \\\\ {2}^{6} & = \\\\ 64\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485459 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485551 1069 "q-177656" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485551 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485551 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a square matrix\n\nII. $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485551 1072 #uuid "83aaff63-f033-5323-bd52-af10976e5a16" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485551 1074 "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I,$ our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485552 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485378 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485552 1069 "q-106240" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485552 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485552 1071 "A survey of adult citizens of a particular city showed that $32%$ of all females and  $5%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m. $51%$ of the population are female. If a person who doesn't eat after $6$ p.m. is selected at random, what is the probability of them being male? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485552 1072 #uuid "8763b60a-8a9e-5a4f-9933-1a229bcc9ffb" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485552 1074 "Let $N$ be the event that a randomly selected person doesn't eat after $6$ p.m., and let $M$ be the event that they are male. Then, we require $P(M|N),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|N)=\\frac{P(N|M)P(M)}{P(N)}=\\frac{P(N|M)P(M)}{P(N|M)P(M)+P(N|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since $32%$ of all females and  $5%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m, we have\n\n$$\nP(N|M)=0.05,\\,P(N|{M}^{′})=0.32.\n$$\n\n- Since $51%$ of the population are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(M)=1-0.51=0.49,\\,P({M}^{′})=0.51.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|N) & =\\frac{P(N|M)P(M)}{P(N|M)P(M)+P(N|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.05)(0.49)}{(0.05)(0.49)+(0.32)(0.51)} \\\\  & ≈13%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485377 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485553 1069 "q-72682" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485553 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485553 1071 "Simplify the  expression\n${q}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{q}^{3}}{{q}^{8}}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485553 1072 #uuid "8ac0604e-5343-56c6-8dd6-8c555b217bdc" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485553 1074 "First we apply the quotient rule, subtracting exponents in the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{q}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{q}^{3}}{{q}^{8}} & = \\\\ {q}^{2}\\cdot {q}^{3-8} & = \\\\ {q}^{2}\\cdot {q}^{-5} & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we apply the product rule, adding exponents in the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{q}^{2}\\cdot {q}^{-5} & = \\\\ {q}^{2+(-5)} & = \\\\ {q}^{2-5} & = \\\\ {q}^{-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{{q}^{3}} & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{q}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{q}^{3}}{{q}^{8}}=\\frac{1}{{q}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485388 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485554 1069 "q-4031" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485554 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485554 1071 "$10\\sqrt{3}-7\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{2}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485554 1072 #uuid "8bd82d37-8d46-5290-9706-952c92700eeb" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485554 1074 "We can combine the two $\\sqrt{3}$ terms together, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\sqrt{3}-7\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{2} & = \\\\ (10-7)\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{2} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485555 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485431 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485555 1069 "q-51802" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485555 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485555 1071 "${(5w)}^{3}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485555 1072 #uuid "8c05d166-8209-505e-9ddb-93264370350e" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485555 1074 "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(5w)}^{3} & = \\\\ {5}^{3}\\cdot {w}^{3} & = \\\\ 125{w}^{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485455 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485556 1069 "q-213447" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485556 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485556 1071 "$\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{7\\sqrt{2}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485556 1072 #uuid "8e0ee09c-000d-5a57-8be4-46c85b5f432d" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485556 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{2}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{2}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{7\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{7\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{7\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{7\\cdot 2} & = \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{14} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2\\cdot 3}}{7} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485557 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485416 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485557 1069 "q-68834" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485557 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485557 1071 "$\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{3}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485557 1072 #uuid "9207bda9-62e5-54ee-9184-105f95fd4290" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485557 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{3}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{3}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{10}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485465 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485558 1069 "q-42556" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485558 1071 "A laboratory test for a particular allergy is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $98%$ of allergy sufferers and $4%$ of non-allergic patients tested positive for the allergy. Only $2%$ of the entire population has the allergy. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested positive is an allergy sufferer? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485558 1072 #uuid "93a07e59-f865-5d60-89bb-f9fb0b96134d" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485558 1074 "Let $A$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is an allergy sufferer, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P(A|T),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(A|T)=\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T)}=\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of a person who has the allergy  testing positive is $98%,$ and the probability of a  non-allergic person testing positive is $4%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(T|A)=0.98,\\,P(T|{A}^{′})=0.04.\n$$\n\n- Since only $2%$ of the population suffers the allergy, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.02,\\,P({A}^{′})=1-0.02=0.98.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|T) & =\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.98)(0.02)}{(0.98)(0.02)+(0.04)(0.98)} \\\\  & ≈0.33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485413 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485559 1069 "q-75663" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485559 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485559 1071 "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $4\\times 4$ matrices, and ${I}_{4}$ is the $4\\times 4$ identity matrix,  which of the following statements are always true?\n\nI. $A{I}_{4}={I}_{4}A$\n\nII. $(CA)B=C(AB)$\n\nIII. $BC=CB$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485559 1072 #uuid "96d290b8-46cc-5702-9916-bb75bce9f305" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485559 1074 "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, however, multiplication by the identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  $A{I}_{4}={I}_{4}A=A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is asociative, so $(CA)B=C(AB)$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $BC\\ne CB$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements I  and II are true." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485379 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485560 1069 "q-105980" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485560 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485560 1071 "Given that $P(D|E)=0.5,$ $P(D)=0.4,$ and $P(E)=0.1,$ compute $P(E|D).$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485560 1072 #uuid "9823650d-b646-5b44-9eff-2feb134e0f44" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485560 1074 "Using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(E|D) & =\\frac{P(D|E)P(E)}{P(D)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.5)(0.1)}{0.4} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.05}{0.4} \\\\  & =0.125.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485561 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485440 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485561 1069 "q-96367" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485561 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485561 1071 "Mark runs a hotel near a popular tourist resort. He knows that, on average, people usually book their room $3.5$ days before their trip. By modeling the number of days between the customer's booking and arrival as continuous and assuming that these numbers are independent, determine the probability that the next customer books their room less than $1$ full day in advance. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485561 1072 #uuid "99aa1c5a-eed9-5ae6-b8c6-e2b13db7c0f6" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485561 1074 "Since the time interval between the customer's booking and arrival is continuous and the time of different bookings are independent and occur at a constant average rate, we can model the time between the next customer's booking and arrival using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of days between a booking and the customer's arrival. Since customers book their room $3.5=7/2$ days in advance on average, $X$ can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =2/7,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{2}{7}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{7}{e}^{-2x/7}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a customer books a room less than $1$ full day in advance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{7}{e}^{-2x/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x/7}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2/7}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.2485\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485562 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485385 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485562 1069 "q-5792" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485562 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485562 1071 "Two identical bins contain chips. The first bin contains four green and two yellow chips. The second bin has three green and one yellow chip. A bin is chosen at random, and a chip is selected from the chosen bin, also at random. If we know that the selected chip is green, what is the probability that it came from the second bin?" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485562 1072 #uuid "9db6fca8-e341-510d-8f9f-24e493c62cfd" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485562 1074 "Let $S$ be the event that the second bin is selected, and let $G$ be the event that a randomly selected chip is green. Then, we require $P(S|G),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem. \n\n$$\nP(S|G)=\\frac{P(G|S)P(S)}{P(G)}=\\frac{P(G|S)P(S)}{P(G|S)P(S)+P(G|{S}^{′})P({S}^{′})}\n$$\n\nNote that ${S}^{′}$ is the event that the first bin is selected.\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the first bin contains four green and two yellow chips, and the second bin contains three green and one yellow chip, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|S)=\\frac{3}{4},\\,P(G|{S}^{′})=\\frac{4}{6}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since the bin is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(S)=P({S}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S|G) & =\\frac{P(G|S)P(S)}{P(G|S)P(S)+P(G|{S}^{′})P({S}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{(\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{(\\frac{17}{12})} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485563 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485461 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485563 1069 "q-105142" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485563 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485563 1071 "${({z}^{6})}^{-3}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485563 1072 #uuid "9fff3a3a-6d2c-53a9-a9af-98e3c6fa167d" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485563 1074 "We multiply the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({z}^{6})}^{-3} & = \\\\ {z}^{6\\cdot (-3)} & = \\\\ {z}^{-18}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485390 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485564 1069 "q-105988" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485564 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485564 1071 "Given that $P(E|G)=0.15,$ $P(E)=0.75,$ and $P(G)=0.3,$ compute $P(G|E).$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485564 1072 #uuid "a0c5c51a-9ae7-508b-bfcb-a9d35170be46" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485564 1074 "Using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G|E) & =\\frac{P(E|G)P(G)}{P(E)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.15)(0.3)}{0.75} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.045}{0.75} \\\\  & =0.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485425 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485565 1069 "q-99624" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485565 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485565 1071 "$\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{13}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485565 1072 #uuid "a64a909b-05fd-57b7-b6a0-ceee0bbc9acd" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485565 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{13}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{13}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{13}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{13}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{\\sqrt{13}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6\\sqrt{13}}{(\\sqrt{13})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485566 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485372 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485566 1069 "q-134546" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485566 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485566 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{500}+4\\sqrt{10}-2\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485566 1072 #uuid "a9e25ac2-f767-5781-99ee-621462efc511" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485566 1074 "First, we use the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{500} & =\\sqrt{100\\cdot 5}=\\sqrt{100}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=10\\sqrt{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{500}+4\\sqrt{10}-2\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 10\\sqrt{5}+4\\sqrt{10}-2\\sqrt{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt{5}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\sqrt{5}+4\\sqrt{10}-2\\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ (10\\sqrt{5}-2\\sqrt{5})+4\\sqrt{10} & = \\\\ (10-2)\\sqrt{5}+4\\sqrt{10} & = \\\\ 8\\sqrt{5}+4\\sqrt{10} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485400 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485567 1069 "q-72665" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485567 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485567 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{{z}^{-8}}{{z}^{-2}}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485567 1072 #uuid "aa08d046-87b1-535b-9f52-33c08a805a92" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485567 1074 "We subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{-8}}{{z}^{-2}} & = \\\\ {z}^{-8-(-2)} & = \\\\ {z}^{-8+2} & = \\\\ {z}^{-6} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485418 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485568 1069 "q-133806" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485568 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485568 1071 "Of the $2$ of Darryl's cherry pies, Ellen takes $\\frac{5}{7}$ of a pie. As a mixed number, Darryl has{{field-1}}\ncherry pies left." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485568 1072 #uuid "aa1b20cc-21de-5821-9525-46da1d460229" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485568 1074 "To find out how much cherry pie Darryl has left, we must calculate the difference $2-\\frac{5}{7}.$\n\nTo subtract $\\frac{5}{7}$ from $2,$ we need to express the whole number $2$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $7.$\n\nFirst, we write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $7$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $7:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\times 7}{1\\times 7}=\\frac{14}{7}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{14}{7}-\\frac{5}{7}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{14}{7}-\\frac{5}{7}=\\frac{14-5}{7}=\\frac{9}{7}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n9÷7=1\\,\\text{R}\\,2=1\\,\\frac{2}{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Darryl has $1\\,\\frac{2}{7}$ cherry pies left." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485569 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485415 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485569 1069 "q-42566" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485569 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485569 1071 "In a particular school, $65%$ of students attend a history course, and $55%$ of students attend a language course.  If $45%$ of language students attend a history course, what is the probability that a randomly chosen history student also attends a language course? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485569 1072 #uuid "b2713c99-1478-532d-bcdd-d19b74d5beb0" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485569 1074 "Let $H$ be the event that a randomly selected student attends a history course, and let $L$ be the event that they attend a language course. Then, we require $P(L|H),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(L|H)=\\frac{P(H|L)P(L)}{P(H)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(H|L)=0.45$ since $45%$ of language students also attend a history course.\n\n- $P(L)=0.55$, since $55%$ of students attend a language course.\n\n- $P(H)=0.65,$ since $65%$ of students attend a history course.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(L|H) & =\\frac{P(H|L)P(L)}{P(H)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.45)(0.55)}{0.65} \\\\  & ≈0.38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485439 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485570 1069 "q-5791" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485570 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485570 1071 "Two identical boxes contain marbles. The first box contains two black and three red marbles. The second box contains four black and three red marbles. A box is selected at random, and one marble is chosen from the selected box, also at random. If the selected marble is red, what is the probability that it is from the first box?" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485570 1072 #uuid "b40352ca-72af-5209-909f-bc2acbfcdd56" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485570 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that the first box is selected, and let $R$ be the event that a randomly selected marble is red. Then, we require $P(F|R),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem. \n\n$$\nP(F|R)=\\frac{P(R|F)P(F)}{P(R)}=\\frac{P(R|F)P(F)}{P(R|F)P(F)+P(R|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})}\n$$\n\nNote that ${F}^{′}$ is the event that the second box is selected.\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the first box contains two black and three red marbles, and the second box contains four black and three red marbles, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|F)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,P(R|{F}^{′})=\\frac{3}{7}.\n$$\n\n- Since the box is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=P({F}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|R) & =\\frac{P(R|F)P(F)}{P(R|F)P(F)+P(R|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5})}{(\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{3}{7})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485430 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485571 1069 "q-254586" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485571 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485571 1071 "A fisherman catches fish continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $1$ fish every $15$ minutes. If the fisherman has already been fishing for $30$ minutes without catching any fish, what is the probability that he will need to fish for at least $20$ more minutes before catching his first fish? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485571 1072 #uuid "b588bb0d-6d8e-5a2d-ae2c-642f8c01ed06" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485571 1074 "Since catching fish occurs continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next catch using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the time until the next catch, in minutes. Since the fisherman catches $1$ fish every $15$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15}$ fish per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{15}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15},$ $s=30,$ and $t=20.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>30+20\\,|\\,X>30) & =P(X>20) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/15)\\cdot (20)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4/3} \\\\  & ≈0.264.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485463 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485572 1069 "q-36836" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485572 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485572 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=5{y}^{2}-2x$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3⟩,$ respectively." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485572 1072 #uuid "b6c8dce9-3855-5452-ba32-6512e799f65b" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485572 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=5{y}^{2}-2x$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3)-f(0,2) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,3⟩-⟨0,2⟩) \\\\ (5(3)^{2}-2(2))-(5(2)^{2}-2(0)) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-2,10y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-2,10{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =⟨-2,10{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =-4+10{c}_{2} \\\\ 10{c}_{2} & =25 \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{3-2} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ x & =2y-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2{c}_{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2},$ we obtain that ${c}_{1}=2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}-4=1.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(1,\\frac{5}{2}).$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485472 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485573 1069 "q-212957" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485573 1071 "$\\frac{\\sqrt{45}}{\\sqrt{2}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485573 1072 #uuid "b78114a3-4df5-565e-b851-3da12ef84e72" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485573 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{2}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{2}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{45}}{\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{45}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{45}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{45}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we notice that $45$ has a perfect square factor $(9).$ Therefore, we can rewrite $\\sqrt{45}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{45} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9\\cdot 5} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify our original fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{45}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{5\\cdot 2}}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485574 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485481 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485574 1069 "q-321932" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485574 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485574 1071 "What is the remainder when $4{x}^{3}-13{x}^{2}-35x+17$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-3x-10?$\n\nThe remainder is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485574 1072 #uuid "b7b36623-4cad-581d-9fdb-5ce9b684b932" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485574 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4x-1 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-3x-10\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,4{x}^{3}-13{x}^{2}-35x+17\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-40x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}+5x+17 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-{x}^{2}+3x+10}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is $4x-1,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $2x+7.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485449 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485575 1069 "q-132546" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485575 1071 "Vehicles arrive at a crossing continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $10$ seconds. Determine the probability that the next vehicle will arrive at the crossing within the next $3$ seconds. Round your answer to four decimal places." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485575 1072 #uuid "b831793d-1ec0-54c8-869c-6f86de445418" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485575 1074 "Since the time intervals between the arrival of two successive vehicles at the crossing are continuous and independent, and arrivals occur at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next vehicle arrives as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time between the arrival of two successive vehicles at the crossing. Then, since vehicles arrive every $10$ seconds on average, the rate is $\\lambda =1/10$ vehicles per second.\n\nThe time between arrivals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =1/10,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next vehicle will arrive at the crossing within the next $3$ seconds is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-x/10}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3/10}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-3/10} \\\\  & ≈0.2592\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485442 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485576 1069 "q-72644" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485576 1071 "$(-2)^{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(-2)^{3}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485576 1072 #uuid "ba0bf3ae-2fd6-52ea-bcad-b5d5a3e42790" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485576 1074 "First, we write the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(-2)^{3}} & =\\frac{(-2)^{7}}{(-2)^{3}} \\\\  & =(-2)^{7}÷(-2)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{7}÷(-2)^{3} & =(-2)^{7-3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485410 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485577 1069 "q-177661" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485577 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a square matrix\n\nII. $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485577 1072 #uuid "bb8ec734-9246-5393-99e6-34bea8046fc0" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485577 1074 "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I,$ our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485376 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485429 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485478 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485578 1069 "q-320017" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485578 1071 "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485578 1072 #uuid "c4c5958e-629a-595b-ba26-53d736ebe0b2" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485578 1074 "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore,\n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$ gives a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which would require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$ gives a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which requires finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485391 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485579 1069 "q-52131" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485579 1071 "Given the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+1,$ $f(3)=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485579 1072 #uuid "c90c9cf4-1ba5-5ca6-8d2b-ccc36ce67160" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485579 1074 "We substitute $x=x$ into the expression for $f(x)$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ f(3) & =3(3)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =27+1 \\\\  & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(3)=28.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485457 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485580 1069 "q-68968" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485580 1071 "$\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{3}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485580 1072 #uuid "cc2b5ce4-6203-5354-ac08-99ae9c209b74" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485580 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{3}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{3}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6\\sqrt{3}}{3} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485447 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485581 1069 "q-72660" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485581 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{{c}^{-4}}{{c}^{-1}}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485581 1072 #uuid "d33d9ced-1c2f-559b-84d3-41f7cab066b8" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485581 1074 "We subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{c}^{-4}}{{c}^{-1}} & = \\\\ {c}^{-4-(-1)} & = \\\\ {c}^{-4+1} & = \\\\ {c}^{-3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485395 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485582 1069 "q-3321" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485582 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485582 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to  $(-10{p}^{-3})^{-3}?$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485582 1072 #uuid "d79e08d6-96d7-5dc0-acd2-d317f660f1d0" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485582 1074 "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-10{p}^{-3})^{-3} & = \\\\ (-10)^{-3}{({p}^{-3})}^{-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{(-10)^{3}}\\cdot {p}^{(-3)(-3)} & = \\\\ -\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\cdot {p}^{9} & = \\\\ -\\frac{{p}^{9}}{1\\,000} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485446 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485583 1069 "q-2886" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485583 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485583 1071 "$\\frac{{r}^{9}}{{r}^{3}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485583 1072 #uuid "d7c0bf43-3499-50b5-82df-1f82cb1c3e1b" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485583 1074 "We subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{r}^{9}}{{r}^{3}} & = \\\\ {r}^{(9-3)} & = \\\\ {r}^{6} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485408 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485584 1069 "q-68357" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485584 1071 "${2}^{-9}÷{2}^{-5}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485584 1072 #uuid "dade69e9-73ae-574b-b21e-ceb9971eb952" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485584 1074 "To divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n{2}^{-9}÷{2}^{-5}={2}^{(-9)-(-5)}={2}^{-4}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate ${2}^{-4},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $(2)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{2}^{-4}={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}=\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{16}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485475 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485585 1069 "q-99629" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485585 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485585 1071 "$\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{3}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485585 1072 #uuid "deb54481-2dc5-5793-afb9-1c365b9534d3" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485585 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{3}$ from the denominator, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{3}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{7}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{7}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the product rule to simplify the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{7}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{7\\cdot 3}}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485367 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485586 1069 "q-114523" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485586 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485586 1071 "What is the remainder when $3{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}-1$ is divided by $3{x}^{2}+2x-1?$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485586 1072 #uuid "e0e14597-882f-5740-a997-7cf558df262d" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485586 1074 "Notice that the dividend is missing the $x$ term. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing term with a coefficient of zero, as follows:\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}-1=3{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+0x-1\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+2 \\\\  & 3{x}^{2}+2x-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,3{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+0x-1\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,3{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\text{ }6{x}^{2}+0x-1 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{6{x}^{2}+4x-2}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,4x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is ${x}^{2}+2,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $-4x+1.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485482 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485587 1069 "q-325109" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485587 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485587 1071 "What is the remainder of $-8{x}^{4}+18{x}^{3}-13{x}^{2}-12x+8$ divided by $2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x+5?$\n\nThe remainder is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485587 1072 #uuid "e87d282e-0d52-5290-b71b-daa1fd77975b" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485587 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-4x+3 \\\\  & 2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-8{x}^{4}+18{x}^{3}-13{x}^{2}-12x+\\phantom{0}8\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,-\\,8{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-20x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+8x+8 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{6{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+3x+15}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5x-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is $-4x+3,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $5x-7.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485384 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485588 1069 "q-95086" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485588 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485588 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)$ and $r(t)=(t+1)\\,i+2t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485588 1072 #uuid "eabe5e6a-dc85-5118-84d2-9aa9f31a6a23" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485588 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z))\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,j+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t+1,$ $y=2t$ and $z={t}^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,j+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+2\\,j+2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,j+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,k)\\cdot (\\,i+2\\,j+2t\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 1+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 2+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2t(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)(1+t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485460 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485589 1069 "q-42557" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485589 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485589 1071 "In a particular city, about $60%$ of mornings are cloudy and $30%$ of days are rainy. \nIt is known that $40%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings. What is the probability that a randomly selected cloudy morning continues as a rainy day?" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485589 1072 #uuid "edf69e26-0d8e-540c-b142-6a409309f520" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485589 1074 "Let $C$ be the event that a randomly selected morning is cloudy, and let $R$ be the event that a randomly selected day is rainy. Then, we require $P(R|C),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(R|C)=\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)}\n$$\n\n- $P(C)=0.6,$ since $60%$ of mornings are cloudy.\n\n- $P(R)=0.3,$ since $30%$ of days are rainy.\n\n- $P(C|R)=0.4,$ since $40%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|C) & =\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.4\\cdot 0.3}{0.6} \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485389 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485590 1069 "q-133802" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485590 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485590 1071 "Aaron, Beatrice, and Chetna have each baked $2$ cherry pies and $3$ apple pies for the sale. In total, they baked{{field-1}}\npies." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485590 1072 #uuid "f3cfd772-56f0-5f8d-9613-e2a636e3f6c4" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485590 1074 "We need to write and evaluate an expression representing the total number of pies.\n\nSince each of them baked $2$ cherry pies and $3$ apple pies, the number of pies each person made is:\n\n$$\n{{2}_{⏟}}_{\\text{cherry pies}}+{{3}_{⏟}}_{\\text{apple pies}}\n$$\n\nSince *each* person made this number of pies, and there are $3$ people, we must multiply the number of pies by $3.$ Therefore, the total number of pies is:\n\n$$\n{{(2+3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{each person}}\\times {{3}_{⏟}}_{\\text{people}}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operations inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2+3)\\times 3 & =(2+3)\\times 3 \\\\  & =5\\times 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply:\n\n$$\n5\\times 3=15\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485591 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485438 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485591 1069 "q-68333" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485591 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485591 1071 "$\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{3}^{-3}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485591 1072 #uuid "f98810bc-183c-5870-9d6b-3edb2448dbd3" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485591 1074 "First, we write the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{3}^{-3}}={3}^{2}÷{3}^{-3}\n$$\n\nNow, to divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2}÷{3}^{-3} & ={3}^{2-(-3)} \\\\  & ={3}^{2+3} \\\\  & ={3}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485452 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485592 1069 "q-104629" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485592 1071 "Simplify the  expression\n${v}^{9}\\cdot \\frac{{v}^{5}}{{v}^{12}}.$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485592 1072 #uuid "faa7323c-2fa9-5fe0-9081-c51997dba998" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485592 1074 "First we apply the quotient rule, subtracting exponents in the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{v}^{9}\\cdot \\frac{{v}^{5}}{{v}^{12}} & = \\\\ {v}^{9}\\cdot {v}^{5-12} & = \\\\ {v}^{9}\\cdot {v}^{-7} & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we apply the product rule, adding exponents in the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{v}^{9}\\cdot {v}^{-7} & = \\\\ {v}^{9+(-7)} & = \\\\ {v}^{9-7} & = \\\\ {v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{v}^{9}\\cdot \\frac{{v}^{5}}{{v}^{12}}={v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485593 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485424 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485593 1069 "q-133805" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485593 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485593 1071 "Darryl buys $2$ cherry pies and $\\frac{4}{3}$ of an apple pie. How much pie did Darryl buy in total? Express your final answer as a mixed number.\n\nDarryl bought\n{{field-1}}\npies." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485593 1072 #uuid "fb3049fb-d3cf-5bb1-b456-516f986d2262" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485593 1074 "To find out how much pie Darryl bought, we have to calculate the following sum:\n\n$$\n2+\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add $2$ and $\\frac{4}{3},$ we need to express the whole number $2$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $3.$\n\nFirst, we write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $3$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\times 3}{1\\times 3}=\\frac{6}{3}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{6+4}{3}=\\frac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nExpressing our answer as a mixed number, we have\n\n$$\n10÷3=3\\,\\text{R}\\,1=3\\,\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Darryl bought $3\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ pies." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485444 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485594 1069 "q-4397" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485594 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485594 1071 "$\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{5}}=$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485594 1072 #uuid "fc330fed-52a6-53ac-a782-16e92ca6af7f" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485594 1074 "To remove the radical $\\sqrt{5}$ from the denominator, we need to multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{5},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{2(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{2\\cdot 5} & = \\\\ \\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485595 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485448 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485595 1069 "q-140567" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485595 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485595 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n\nI. $U=\\{Q([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{Q([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{Q([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])\\}$" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485595 1072 #uuid "fd59782d-c853-5fe4-812d-f8b2814488a9" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485595 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nLet's denote the elements of $U,W,$ and $V$ respectively as \n\n$\\,$ $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\},\\,$ $\\{Q({v}_{1}),Q({v}_{2}),Q({v}_{3})\\},\\,$ and $\\,\\{Q({w}_{1}),Q({w}_{2})\\}.$\n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2})=0,\\,Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0,\\,Q({v}_{2})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0.$\n\n- $Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2}) & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=-4+25=21\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the Set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]=0+2\\sqrt{5}-2\\sqrt{5}=0,\\,✓ \\\\ Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=-2+0-2=-4.\\,\\times  \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+0+\\sqrt{5}=\\sqrt{5}\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2}) & ={w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=-10+10=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$W$ only\"." 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485596 1058 #edb/ref 17592186485602 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485596 1062 #uuid "01573a3c-c095-5815-9e2c-e98160adf429" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485596 1063 #edb/ref 17592186423671 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485597 1058 #edb/ref 17592186485620 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485597 1062 #uuid "05097e08-aba7-5438-8b05-16aa93723026" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485597 1063 #edb/ref 17592186120356 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485598 1058 #edb/ref 17592186485621 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485598 1062 #uuid "090604db-a13f-5a31-aa02-93d119fcd0b8" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485598 1063 #edb/ref 17592186362602 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485599 1058 #edb/ref 17592186485605 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485599 1062 #uuid "0b1d3568-dd0f-5ab5-a242-44cbf5280b8e" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485599 1063 #edb/ref 17592186485489 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485600 1058 #edb/ref 17592186485632 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485600 1062 #uuid "1005f30a-98c7-5f52-8fb4-cef4933ce2c2" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485600 1063 #edb/ref 17592186485593 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485601 1058 #edb/ref 17592186485610 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485601 1062 #uuid "1140fa5b-f47a-5b21-a507-87491673347d" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485601 1063 #edb/ref 17592186426072 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485602 1058 #edb/ref 17592186485623 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485602 1062 #uuid "12714b70-150c-5e2d-9693-0688dd834525" 13194139535245 17592186045417 true] [17592186485602 1063 #edb/ref 17592186419007 13194139535245 175921